sform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{3-5{e}^{2t}} & =L{3}+L{-5{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =3L{1}-5L{{e}^{2t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the following result:\n\n$$\nL{{e}^{kt}}=\\frac{1}{s-k},\\,s>k\n$$\n\n$$\nL{1}=\\frac{1}{s},\\,s>0\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{1} & =\\frac{1}{s},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{{e}^{2t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-2},\\,s>2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =3L{1}-5L{{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{s}-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{s}-\\frac{5}{s-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(s-2)-5s}{s(s-2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2(s+3)}{s(s-2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $L{1}$ is defined for $s\\in (0,\\infty )$ and $L{{e}^{2t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (2,\\infty ),$ we have that $L{f(t)}$ is defined for\n\n$$\n(0,\\infty )\\cap (2,\\infty )=(2,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nthat is, $s>2.$" 13194139534921 true] [17592186320004 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320005 13194139534921 true] [17592186320004 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534921 true] [17592186320004 1041 "field-1" 13194139534921 true] [17592186320004 1104 #edb/ref 17592186320005 13194139534921 true] [17592186320004 1110 #uuid "0791a5d5-3615-5b0a-a2ec-01635434cc40" 13194139534921 true] [17592186320005 1034 "\\frac{3}{s}-\\frac{5}{s-2}" 13194139534921 true] [17592186320005 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534921 true] [17592186320005 1111 #uuid "d0175c04-e4da-5fc4-ba40-43c65c540cfd" 13194139534921 true] [17592186320006 1017 #edb/ref 17592186320007 13194139534921 true] [17592186320006 1089 "q-331464" 13194139534921 true] [17592186320006 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534921 true] [17592186320006 1100 "Find the Laplace transform of $f(t)=1-3{e}^{4t}$ for $s>4.$\n\n$L{f(t)}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534921 true] [17592186320006 1113 #uuid "7560d20f-6a91-5fd6-b240-b4b3e7d11194" 13194139534921 true] [17592186320006 1145 "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states 